## 0. Core Invariants (never break)

> Architectural constraints this project must "never break." Changes that violate these are rejected outright.

{{INVARIANTS_BLOCK}}

## 1. Core Workflow

**After completing each operation, do these three things in order**:

1. **Update the docs first** — an entry under `docs/Progress.md` "## Changelog" (symptom / root cause / decision / verification result) is mandatory; a fix produced by a review-round rejection/re-review/final verification doesn't open a new entry — append one line ("review round N: …") to the end of the unit's existing entry instead; use the "## 2. Doc Sync Rules" table below to decide whether `docs/PLAN.md` (a Phase advanced / the plan changed), `docs/DECISIONS.md` (key decisions: tech choice, tradeoff, plan reversal), `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`, or `docs/DEPLOYMENT.md` also needs updating
2. **Make one atomic commit that includes both the code and the docs** — `git add` the source changes from this operation together with the doc updates from step 1; the message should make clear what changed and why (always review `git diff --staged` before committing)
3. **Push the current branch immediately** — `git push` the current feature/wip branch right after committing (no confirmation needed; use `-u` to set upstream if there isn't one yet; if there's no remote, follow §5.1 item 3 and remind the user to set one up)

The granularity of "one operation" ≈ the change covered by one atomic commit. Don't lump multiple independent changes into one big commit.

**Monthly changelog archiving**: the "Changelog" section of `docs/Progress.md` only keeps entries from the **current month**; at the first batch wrap-up of each month, cut last month's (and older) entries wholesale into `docs/archive/Progress-<YYYY-MM>.md` (split by the entry's own date, same format as the main file's changelog section, newest first; add a `# Progress Archive — <YYYY-MM>` heading line the first time the file is created), leaving one archive-index line under the main file's "Changelog" heading: `> Older entries are archived monthly in docs/archive/Progress-YYYY-MM.md`. The "Progress Overview" status table is never archived. The archiving action is its own separate `docs:` commit, never mixed with source changes. Reading rules are unchanged: only read the main file's last 2-3 entries when returning to the project; only go to the archive when tracing history.

> **Why "update docs first, then one commit" rather than "commit first, update docs after"** (this order flipped once before — this footnote exists so it doesn't flip again):
> 1. "Commit first, docs after" necessarily produces at least one historical commit where code and docs disagree; if the session gets interrupted right there, you're left in exactly the "wrote code but didn't write docs" end state that Section 5 explicitly forbids;
> 2. Updating the docs is part of the same "operation" — bundling it into one commit is what actually satisfies this section's own "one operation ≈ one atomic commit" rule; splitting it into two commits quietly redefines that granularity;
> 3. Empirical evidence: across multiple real-project smoke runs, docs and code were committed together throughout, and the resulting history stayed clean with no intermediate inconsistent state.

## 2. Doc Sync Rules

| Change type | Files that must be updated |
|---|---|
| New/modified source code | `docs/Progress.md` (implementation record) + `docs/PLAN.md` (phase progress) if needed |
| Bug fix / gotcha encountered | `docs/Progress.md` (symptom, root cause, fix) |
| Phase progresses | `docs/PLAN.md` + `docs/Progress.md` |
| `.env.example` / deploy script changes | `docs/Progress.md` + `docs/DEPLOYMENT.md` |
| Inter-module interface changes (HTTP API / WS payload) | `docs/Progress.md` + `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` (interface table) |
| Dependency changes (go.mod / package.json, etc.) | `docs/Progress.md` (what was added, why) |
| Requirement changes / reversals | `docs/REQUIREMENTS.md` (single source of truth, edit here first; division of labor note: business-fact changes go to BUSINESS, product-scope changes go to REQUIREMENTS) + `docs/Progress.md` |
| Business rule / business process changes | `docs/BUSINESS.md` (source of business facts, edit here first) + `docs/Progress.md` |
| Key decisions (tech choice / tradeoff / plan reversal / workaround) | `docs/DECISIONS.md` (What/Why/Changes) + `docs/Progress.md` |
| Meeting notes | `docs/MEETINGS.md` (raw archive) + `docs/BUSINESS.md` (distilled business facts) + `docs/REQUIREMENTS.md` (distilled product requirements) + `docs/REQUIREMENTS.md` Goal Ledger (requirement-type items) |
| A requirement/expectation/complaint surfaces in conversation | `docs/REQUIREMENTS.md` Goal Ledger (same turn, reply with one confirmation line) |
| Built a reusable component (general-purpose, not business-specific) | An entry in your org's component index (verify real paths first) + `docs/Progress.md` |
| `DECISIONS.inbox` has drafts | Triage before commit (distill decisions into `docs/DECISIONS.md`, requirements into the `docs/REQUIREMENTS.md` goal inbox, discard noise) |

**Principle**: docs are the single source of truth. When code and docs disagree, decide which one is correct, then bring the other into line.

## 3. Post-Change Quality Checks

1. Read through the changed file once to confirm the logic is correct
2. Grep related docs for stale info (old function names, field names, paths) and update them
3. Make sure code snippets / config examples in the docs match the actual code
4. UTF-8 mojibake scan: `LC_ALL=C grep -l $'\xef\xbf\xbd' docs/*.md .claude/*.md`
5. When a change affects `.env.example`, confirm the local `.env` (not in git) is synced too
6. After a frontend/interaction change, run a ui-sweep (interaction smoke walkthrough); once it's done, log one sweep record to `docs/Progress.md` (name `ui-sweep` explicitly, plus date + how many screens/clicks scanned + outcome — log it even with 0 defects); log real defects separately, one changelog entry each (symptom/root cause/fix); for destructive buttons outside the block list, extend the project's DENY_EXTRA first, then run it
7. Processes started for tests, verification, or screenshots (dev servers, mock services, headless browsers, temporary containers) are shut down as soon as testing ends — also when it fails or is abandoned midway; record the PID / port / session name at startup and confirm each one has exited before reporting done (child processes spawned through a wrapper command such as `npm` / `npx` are shut down and checked too); shut down only what you started yourself, never mass-kill by name with `pkill -f` / `killall`, and only list and report leftover processes someone else left behind
8. Before running tests in parallel, load tests, or several parallel units, check CPU usage first (macOS `top -l 2 -n 0 -s 1`, Linux `top -bn2 -d1`; read the last CPU line — `CPU usage` on macOS, `%Cpu(s)` on Linux; usage = 100 − idle; memory via `memory_pressure` on macOS, `free -m` on Linux): above 70% halve the current concurrency (minimum 1); above 90%, or with less than 20% memory available, run serially (a review chain's blind round may run sequentially too — the reviewers still cannot see each other's conclusions); only when `top` gives no reading, fall back to the 1-minute `uptime` load against the core count (`sysctl -n hw.ncpu` on macOS, `nproc` on Linux; above 70% of the cores halve, above the core count go serial); the report states the CPU usage, available memory and the concurrency actually used
9. For changes touching the request path, scheduled / background jobs, external calls, or health checks, check every added or changed log line against the log contract (§4): stable `msg`, all fields present, no secrets or personal data
10. If the change leaves headless background processes behind at runtime, at deploy time, or in the session (headless browsers, child processes, long-lived workers, scheduled jobs, background containers or daemons; processes left in the session by `nohup`, `&`, `docker run -d`, `tmux` / `screen`, agent-browser sessions, batch `claude -p`), confirm a time-based cleanup that does not depend on whoever started them is in place: a timer inside the service sweeps against the registry every few minutes and, at startup, first clears what its own last crash left behind, or a host sweeps with cron, systemd timer, or launchd; for things started in a session, put the time limit on the command that actually runs long (`timeout -k 30s <duration> <command>`, on macOS install coreutils and use `gtimeout`; with `tmux`, check after launching that the process is really running; containers from `docker run -d` get a label and the host cleans them by label and creation time, and only one-shot containers with no restart policy may use `--rm` plus a `timeout` inside the container), not on a client that returns immediately; only processes registered by the same service, deployment, or session count (process group, PID file, container label, session name), before terminating check that the start time and command line still match what was registered, and if not delete only the registry entry; never terminate in bulk by name; every process has a maximum lifetime and the number alive at once has a cap, past either close gracefully first and if that fails force-kill the whole process group, and the round with cleanup actions logs `job end` under the log contract (a round with no action logs only `job heartbeat`); a container's `init`, memory and process-count limits, and restart policy only prevent resource exhaustion and cannot replace the scheduled sweep. Processes closed on the spot during tests follow item 7. Where it truly cannot be set up, state the reason and how to check by hand

## 4. Code Conventions

{{CODE_CONVENTIONS_BLOCK}}

**Logging (observability contract, write it this way from day one)**:
- **Shape**: one JSON object per line, written to stdout (containerized services are collected and rotated by the runtime; do not write log files); every line has `time` (RFC3339, milliseconds), `level` (`debug` / `info` / `warn` / `error`, lowercase), and `msg` (a stable short phrase with no variables embedded; variables always go in separate fields). `msg` is the interface: monitoring and troubleshooting recognize lines by it, so changing it changes the interface. One line is at most 4 KB; put stack traces in a `stack` field.
- **Required event lines** (`msg` is fixed; these English phrases are not translated or reworded):
  - Before the process starts listening, `service start` (`service`, `version`, `addr`); on process exit, `service stop` (`reason`, plus `err` on failure).
  - When each HTTP request ends, `http request` (`method`, `route` as the route template rather than the actual path, `path`, `status`, `duration_ms`, `request_id`, plus `user_id` when logged in; error level for 5xx; health-check paths are not logged).
  - On a caught panic / unhandled exception, `panic` (`err`, `stack`, `request_id`; if the response was already written, only log the line and abort the connection).
  - Scheduled / background jobs with an interval > 1 minute: `job start` at the beginning (`job`, `run_id`, `trigger` = timer / manual / retry / startup) and `job end` at the end (`job`, `run_id`, `ok`, `duration_ms`, per-kind counts, `err` on failure; a partial failure is also `ok=false`).
  - High-frequency polling with an interval <= 1 minute (once a minute counts; polling that only checks whether there is work follows this rule regardless of interval): log `job end` only when something was done or an error occurred, and emit one `job heartbeat` line every 5 minutes (`job`, `ticks`, counts since the last heartbeat, `skipped`, `errors`).
  - Job retries, `job retry` (`job`, `attempt`, `max_attempts`, `next_retry_in_s`, at warn; on giving up, at error with `attempts` and `giving_up=true`).
  - Calls to external services (LLM, third-party API, database migration), `<target> call`, e.g. `llm call`, at info, warn / error on failure (`target`, `ok`, `status` (HTTP status code; 0 or null if unavailable), `duration_ms`, `attempt` or `attempts`; never log the prompt, request body, response body, or tokens).
- **Dedup**: when the same cause keeps failing, log the first error with a given error text only; afterwards drop to debug or carry a `repeat` count; reset once it recovers. Never repeat the identical error every N minutes. Dedup applies only to repeated error-description lines for the same cause; `http request` and `<target> call` lines are logged every time, at their normal level.
- **Health checks**: `GET /health` has liveness semantics, always HTTP 200, with body `{ok, version, db, uptime_s}` and `ok=false` when a dependency is broken — the container health check probes it (the process being alive means 200, so a brief database hiccup does not get the container marked unhealthy); when readiness semantics are needed (for example before switching traffic in a blue-green cutover), add a separate `GET /health/ready` that returns 503 when a dependency is unavailable, and change the pre-switch probe path in the deployment config to it. Neither path writes an access line.
- **Never log**: secrets, tokens, cookies, Authorization headers, passwords, raw emails and phone numbers (mask them if you must, `t***@domain`), user-authored content, LLM prompts and response bodies. Error text counts too: `fmt.Errorf("lookup user (%s)", email)`, which splices an email into the error, violates this just the same.
- **Version injection**: Dockerfile `ARG APP_VERSION=unknown` + `ENV APP_VERSION=$APP_VERSION`, and CI builds pass `--build-arg APP_VERSION=<short commit hash>`; at runtime the version is taken from `APP_VERSION`, then the vcs revision in the build info, then `unknown`.

See the "Log contract" section of `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` for the full table and the acceptance checklist.

**General**:
- UTF-8; comments may be written in the team's working language
- With multiple modules: modules **must not import each other** — communicate only via HTTP / WS (preserve module boundaries)

## 5. Git Commit Conventions

- Commit message first line ≤ 72 characters, prefixed with `feat:` / `fix:` / `docs:` / `refactor:` / `test:` / `chore:`
- Any language is fine; keep the language consistent across commits
- Split large changes into atomic commits
- Always review `git diff --staged` before committing
- **Never commit**: `.env`, real credentials, `node_modules/`, `data/` (private), `*.db`
- **A conversation session must never end in a "wrote code but didn't write docs" state**

### 5.1 Branch & Backup Strategy

1. **Branch means push**: day-to-day development always happens on a feature/wip branch — switch to or create one before starting work (a worktree flow satisfies this automatically); push the current branch to the remote immediately after every commit, no confirmation needed — this is your real-time offsite backup
2. **main is gated — only for changes the user can see in the frontend**: before merging into main (or committing directly on main), ask one question — could the user see or test the difference from the frontend? **Yes** (frontend code changes, or backend logic changes that alter frontend interaction/display behavior) → get the user's confirmation first, attaching the changed page for inspection (merge all screenshots into one self-contained HTML — images embedded, opens directly in a browser, one caption line per image — deliver it in the conversation and print its local file path so it can be pasted into a browser; a text description alone or loose screenshots don't count; before delivering to the user, run a rendered scan `npx impeccable@<pinned-version> detect --json <address>` on the screenshot address (hand its output to the visual-review subagent together with the screenshots; if it cannot run, record "detect not run"), then dispatch a visual-review subagent (`'opus'` + `'medium'`) to inspect every screenshot one by one, hunting the defects anyone would spot at a glance — broken layout, overlapping elements, overflowing or clipped text, mojibake or placeholder text, blank or data-less regions, obviously missing styles, error messages; fix what it finds, re-screenshot, re-review, and only deliver once the review passes; when unsure whether something is a defect or intentional design, don't force a fix — flag it in the reply for the user to decide, and include a one-line review verdict (pass, or what was fixed); if the app cannot be run or screenshots cannot be produced, stop and report the gate as blocked — never degrade to a text-only confirmation); **No** (anything the user can't test from the frontend: pure backend internals and refactors, docs, backend tests, scripts, CI, dependency bumps, changes that leave API behavior unchanged) → merge and push main directly, no confirmation needed — just report the merge in one line. Either way, delete the merged remote feature branch; when unsure, treat it as "yes" and ask; committing directly on main is still governed by rule 1 (branch first) — this clause only decides whether to ask. This clause overrides finishing-a-development-branch's Step 4 three-option menu — on a "no" verdict, skip the menu, merge, and continue the wrap-up
3. **No-remote fallback**: if the repo has no remote (or the branch has no upstream), remind the user to set one up after the first commit, so "auto-push" doesn't silently fail
4. **Delete worktrees when done**: only create them at `.worktrees/<branch-name>` inside the repo (must be in .gitignore) or the native EnterWorktree location `.claude/worktrees/`; never in a sibling directory (`xxx-wt-*`) or `/tmp`, including for one-off merge/audit uses; once merged, abandoned, or the one-off use ends, in the same wrap-up `git worktree remove` + `git worktree prune` + delete the local branch if it's merged into main, and say "worktrees cleaned" in the wrap-up report; clean up every worktree you created this session regardless of where it lives (this overrides finishing-a-development-branch's "only clean up under `.worktrees/`" limit); before wrapping up a batch, run `git worktree list` to audit and remove anything not part of the in-flight batch; leave alone (and list for the user) any worktree with uncommitted or unmerged content
5. **Squash review rounds into the unit commit**: a unit's first commit is `feat:`/`fix:` as usual; fixes produced afterward by review rejection, re-review, or final verification are committed with `git commit --squash=<the unit's first commit sha> -m "review round N: <what changed>"` (still push immediately after every commit as before — the backup habit doesn't change). Before merging into main, ahead of the gate decision, the main conversation runs `GIT_EDITOR=true git rebase --autosquash main` on the wip branch to squash each round into its unit commit (the round notes land automatically in the commit body), then `git push --force-with-lease origin <wip branch>`, then merges as usual. `--force-with-lease` is only allowed at this one step, only for its own wip branch — main and shared branches still forbid force push, unchanged, and the implementation agent's red line stands (this step is done by the main conversation at wrap-up, never dispatched). Fixes that come from real-machine/production feedback after a unit is already merged into main still get their own independent `fix:` commit as usual — that's genuine rework and should stay visible in the stats. Review rounds also don't open a new changelog entry in `docs/Progress.md`; they get appended as one line — "review round N: …" — at the end of that unit's existing entry

## 6. Secrets Handling

**Real values that must never be written into md files or code** (API keys, tokens, passwords, encryption keys, etc.) are all read from `.env` (not in git). `.env.example` uses placeholder values.

## 7. Planning & Doc Conventions (tied to brainstorming / ultracode)

- Design specs produced by brainstorming go to `docs/superpowers/specs/YYYY-MM-DD-<topic>-design.md`; the spec includes a section "Easy-to-miss inputs and failure modes" (up to five inputs or failure conditions that the spec implies but that no acceptance criterion names and no test would exercise, each stating what a reasonable user would expect, most likely to cause trouble first; each gets one test in the unit that owns the code, attached to that unit's dispatch prompt and checked by reviewers; if you checked and found none, say so in one line); for a showcase frontend: when there is no PRODUCT.md the main conversation first runs `/impeccable init` (whatever the task); for a new page or change of visual style the main conversation then runs `/impeccable shape`, hands the direction card and composition comps to the user to pick and approve, and writes the direction and approved-comp paths into the spec; local extensions and polishing existing pages skip shape and go straight to a subagent
- **Once the spec is written, implement it directly from the spec via ultracode (Workflow multi-agent orchestration) — skip writing-plans / an implementation-plan document**
- **Small changes (brainstorming's bounded path) also get a small spec and are implemented as soon as it is written — don't wait for the user's confirmation.** This rule and the one above override brainstorming's "no spec, stop for approval" requirement and its whole HARD-GATE block (as of superpowers 6.4.1 it lists, per path, the approvals required before implementation: for the bounded path, approval of the short in-chat design; for the architectural path, approval of the written spec, then a review of the written implementation plan and a choice of execution method), including its sentence "A reply approves the stage actually presented": writing the spec authorizes every stage after it, with no stage-by-stage check-ins; the spike path stays as brainstorming defines it
- Once the spec is written, add a line under "## Spec Index" in `docs/PLAN.md`: `- [<topic>](superpowers/specs/<date>-<topic>-design.md) — <one-liner>`; PLAN.md stays a roadmap index and doesn't carry body content
- When writing-plans / subagent-driven / inline execution is explicitly named, follow the named approach
- Write specs, dispatch prompts, code comments, and commit messages for security/hardening work in neutral engineering language describing **what the system does** (validation, rate limiting, expiry, tightened permissions), not what attack it defends against; record threat scenarios in `docs/DECISIONS.md`, not in dispatch prompts or code comments — this avoids a content-safety classifier blanket-blocking the message and forcing a rewrite
- **Restate before acting**: for every requirement the user raises (a logic change, a new feature, a bug fix, an investigation — all count), once you understand it, the first line of your reply must be "Got it, next I'll do <a concrete one-line restatement>"; number them if there are several, one line each; fold this into the same line as the Goal Ledger log ("Got it, next I'll do X; logged to the Goal Ledger"), and only start reading files or making changes after the restatement; be concrete about the action and object ("fix the email-sending logic"), not just "Got it"; if you don't understand, ask directly rather than papering over it with a vague restatement
- **Put anything the user must type in the command line into the clipboard first**: for what the user must type themselves in a terminal or the session input box (a command, a `!`-prefixed command, a slash command such as `/goal`, a command to run for login), the main conversation, before the reply that gives it (after the restatement line), first puts it into the clipboard verbatim and reads it back to check it character by character; the reply still gives the code block and says "put into the clipboard"; first write the text into a private temp file with single quotes or a quoted heredoc (`printf '%s' '<text>' > "$f"`), then put it with `LC_ALL=en_US.UTF-8 pbcopy < "$f"` and compare byte for byte with `LC_ALL=en_US.UTF-8 pbpaste | cmp -s - "$f"`; never use `echo "…"` (so `$VAR` and `$(…)` are not expanded or executed locally first) and leave no trailing newline in the clipboard; on Linux use `wl-copy` / `wl-paste -n` or `xclip -selection clipboard`, on Windows / WSL use PowerShell `Set-Clipboard` / `Get-Clipboard -Raw`; several commands to run in the same place are combined into one block, and for things to paste in different places put only the one needed first and say which; for secrets put only a placeholder or the form that reads an environment variable, never an authorization code or token itself; when the user says the value is in the clipboard, first save it to a private temp file (`umask 077`, under `$TMPDIR`, never echo the value, delete it after use) and then put it in, and when the flow itself has the user paste a value from the clipboard, do not put anything this round and say so; subagents do not touch the clipboard; when the session runs on a remote machine or it cannot be put in, write "clipboard unavailable: <reason>" and do not pretend it was put in
- **Multi-agent division of labor (applies to ultracode and any other parallel dispatch)**: the main conversation (strongest model, e.g. Fable/Opus) acts as Conductor — breaks down tasks, sets the approach, writes the Workflow script, does the final rollup and conflict adjudication; everything else is dispatched to subagents to execute. **Every stage's `model` and `effort` must be written explicitly** (the verdict round is no exception: write `model: 'opus'`) — omitting `model` inherits the main session's model, and omitting `effort` inherits the main session's tier (the main session often runs at xhigh, so omitting it means mechanical work runs at xhigh depth too):

  | Stage type | model | effort |
  |---|---|---|
  | Locating files / listing / inventory | `'haiku'` (first choice; switch to `'sonnet'` only when it fails) | `'low'` |
  | Batch mechanical work: migration, renaming, templated code changes | `'sonnet'` | `'low'` |
  | Regular implementation (TDD units) | `'sonnet'` | `'medium'` |
  | Hard implementation: algorithms, concurrency, tricky bugs | `'sonnet'` | `'high'` |
  | Frontend visuals / UI polish ("does it look right" work; pure-logic frontend doesn't count) | `'opus'` | `'medium'` |
  | Blind round of a review chain (first round: 2–3 agents, none seeing the others, parallel, no vote counting) and dig rounds (carry every finding; refute first, then fill gaps); principle-3 units run every round at `'high'` | `'opus'` | `'medium'` |
  | Verdict round of a review chain (the last round; may be skipped when the blind round has nothing at P0/P1 and no contradictions; mandatory for principle-3 units): final review, acceptance verdict, security-related review | `'opus'` | `'high'` |
  | Architectural judgment, tradeoff decisions, final rollup | not dispatched, stays with the main conversation | — |

  - **Escalate on failure — don't pay up front**: implementation stages always start at `'medium'`; only escalate to `'high'` when a unit is bounced back by tests or review and rerun
  - **`'xhigh'` / `'max'` never go into batch stages** — reserve them for single-point judgments where "getting it wrong once is expensive"; if a review needs more confidence, add rounds rather than escalating a single round, and never count votes — blind-round agents don't see each other, start from zero and use fixed lenses (correctness; boundary and malformed data; security for the third), every dig round switches to a lens not used before (security, spec acceptance-criteria coverage, integration seams, rollback and reversibility, adversarial refutation), the tests themselves are checked every round, and no round rereads the same prompt; a same-lens round is a round that never happened
  - **Consequence overrides difficulty**: units touching data migrations, deleting/overwriting data, auth and permissions, payments, or public releases get `'opus'` + `'high'` on every round of the review chain (3 blind reviewers) with a mandatory verdict round — a blind round alone may not pass them — no matter how low their implementation tier is, and the checklist must include "a rollback path exists and is executable"
  - Before entering ultracode, first output a 3-5 line kickoff summary (unit count, each unit's tier, estimated scale) — don't wait for confirmation, start right away; it just gives the user a visible interrupt window
  - Attach a review stage (blind, dig and verdict rounds all on `opus`) after every unit that writes code (stream them one by one via `pipeline`, don't fence-wait to line them all up); the review agent must actually read the changed files to verify — it must not just trust the implementer's report — and must also examine the tests themselves and the log contract (§4) (do they cover the acceptance criteria; do they only test the happy path; for units that start external processes, are the shutdown requirements and the scheduled cleanup (§3 item 10) implemented and tested); weak tests count as a rejection, and a rejected unit's re-run splits tests and implementation across two agents; behavior the spec does not mention is judged by what a reasonable user would expect (the spec's silence is not permission to fail; severity follows the effect on that user)
  - Review orchestration is always blind start + chained digging + verdict: the first round dispatches 2 review agents (3 for principle-3 high-risk units, every round at `'high'`) in parallel, none seeing the others, each from scratch on a fixed lens (correctness; boundary and malformed data; the third adds security; checking the tests themselves is done every round and takes no lens slot), with no vote counting — every agent returns the same schema findings[{file, line, severity: P0|P1|P2, claim, lens}], the script merges by file+line (repo-relative path, start line, ±3 lines count as the same spot; what can't be normalised goes to the next round) and marks same-location entries with opposite conclusions or different severities as conflict, semantic contradictions go to the next round; every later round is one agent whose prompt explicitly carries every earlier finding (confirmed items, overturned items, re-verified checkpoints), refutes first and then fills gaps on a lens not used before (in order: security, spec acceptance-criteria coverage, integration seams, rollback and reversibility, adversarial refutation), reporting only new findings and reversals; the last round is the verdict round, on `'opus'` + `'high'` (write `model` explicitly), blind and dig rounds are `'medium'`. Routing: only P2 → pass and record as leftovers; nothing at P0/P1 but a conflict → no fix, straight to a verdict round; P1 → fix, then verdict round; P0 → fix, then one dig round, then verdict; a verdict round with new P0/P1 → fix and route the same way, counting toward the cap; stop when the verdict round has no new P0/P1 and nothing at P0/P1 is open (closed = fixed and confirmed by a later round), default cap 4 rounds (the blind round counts as one) and going past it needs a stated reason. Fixes never happen inside the review stage: as soon as a P0/P1 needs fixing this run is over for the unit, the main conversation dispatches implementation agents through the rejection flow and then starts a new run that continues the same chain, with the chain's state in `docs/reviews/<unit>-chain.md` (created and appended by the main conversation) whose path the next round's prompt receives and whose round count is inherited. Parallel multi-vote review (independent votes, each from zero, majority decides) is no longer used — every vote rediscovers the same problems and none can catch "the prior round's conclusion itself was wrong"; the blind round only discovers, it counts nothing and rules on nothing; diagnosis / root-cause / troubleshooting chain the same way, each round carrying the prior conclusions and rejected hypotheses, stop after two consecutive rounds with nothing new (same default cap of 4 rounds), no verdict round (unless the conclusion feeds an irreversible action)
  - Review reports state only the delta (new findings and reversals vs. the earlier rounds) — no "checked it, looks fine" summaries; when there's nothing new, say so explicitly and list the checkpoints that were re-verified
  - Beyond the spec excerpt and this file's hard rules, implementer-agent prompts must also name the production red lines and file ownership: the FORBIDDEN FILES list, never restart shared services, never read/write production data, no force push or any history rewrite that discards changes, and touch only the files assigned to this unit; processes started for tests are shut down when testing ends, and CPU usage is checked before running in parallel (§3 items 7 and 8); when several units run in parallel only one unit at a time may run the full or heavy test suite — the prompt states that an implementer agent finishes by running only its own unit's targeted tests (or the short suite), not the project's full suite, and that this instruction takes precedence over the superpowers TDD skill's requirement to run the project's whole test suite before calling a change done (any failure it does see, whether or not its unit caused it, is still reported by name), and the main conversation runs the full suite unit by unit in sequence after the implementers return and hands the results to the reviewers, who do not run the full suite themselves; when the unit's code starts external processes (headless browsers, child processes, long-running workers), the prompt states that shutdown must be implemented: a maximum lifetime after which the whole process group is ended, shutdown that tries graceful first and then forced with a bounded wait, a cap on how many stay alive at once, shutdown on the error / timeout / cancel / startup-failure paths, a timeout on every call, memory and process-count limits for containers, plus a scheduled cleanup that does not depend on whoever started the process (§3 item 10), and tests covering that the processes really exit after a hang or error and that the scheduled cleanup reaps a process that has outlived its limit
  - A frontend-visual stage (`'opus'`) first classifies the work: for a showcase frontend (page facing external users; when unsure, treat as showcase), the main conversation first runs `/impeccable init` when there is no PRODUCT.md; the direction for a new page or a change of style is set by the main conversation with impeccable (the main conversation runs shape, the user picks the direction card and approves the composition comp, and the paths go into the spec), and the subagent only builds from the spec (from new-work section 6 on) or runs `polish`; local extensions and polishing existing pages go straight to a subagent; if the spec lacks a direction the subagent stops and reports it, and where impeccable would ask the user it decides from the spec / PRODUCT.md / DESIGN.md without inventing preferences; without impeccable it uses hallmark / frontend-design, and the direction is likewise set by the main conversation; admin consoles and internal-tool UIs do not invoke one; after the frontend unit returns and before the review chain the main conversation runs `npx impeccable@<pinned-version> detect --json <target>` (rendered scan feeds the hard gate and at-a-glance hits go back first (low-contrast: the main conversation first judges whether it is intentionally subdued secondary text and only otherwise sends it back), source scan is only attached to the review, pattern-type hits are only attached to the review, not run is recorded as "detect not run" and never counts as a pass; art sites first write `detector.ignoreRules` in `.impeccable/config.json` to exempt deliberate layouts); then the prompt names the default styles the project's first pass actually produced so the agent avoids them, extending the list each round; generic "don't look AI-made" instructions do almost nothing on Opus 5.5
  - Per-call `effort` is only supported by Workflow's `agent()` — the bare Agent tool has no such parameter (except agent types whose definition frontmatter sets `effort:`, e.g. `mechanical` is fixed at `low`) → batch and parallel work always goes through Workflow orchestration
  - Pure-mechanical stages (locating/listing; batch migration/renaming/templating) dispatch via `agent(..., { agentType: 'mechanical', effort: 'low' })` — `mechanical` is a subagent shipped in this plugin with `omitClaudeMd: true`, so it skips the auto-loaded CLAUDE.md and saves tokens; because it doesn't load CLAUDE.md, any write-capable mechanical stage must carry the production red lines in its own prompt. Regular implementation and all review agents keep CLAUDE.md.
  - The Workflow per-run concurrent-agent cap defaults to about `min(16, cores-2)`; raise it with the env var `CLAUDE_CODE_WORKFLOW_MAX_CONCURRENT_AGENTS` (1–256) only when the machine has CPU/memory headroom, never on an already-loaded machine; check CPU usage and available memory once before starting a run (§3 item 8), and if it's already high, run fewer parallel units or run them in batches
  - The tier table is a dated empirical calibration — after each major model generation, revisit it against your session usage data; change the table based on data, not vibes

## Never Do

- Change code without updating docs / committing
- Write real credentials into md files
- Commit `data/` into git
- Break the Core Invariants in Section 0
- Merge a change the user can see in the frontend into main/the default branch (via merge or a direct commit) without confirmation — changes with no frontend-visible difference merge directly per §5.1, no asking
- Hear a requirement and not log it to the Goal Ledger (not logging it counts as not having heard it; when unsure, log it as `open` anyway — better to over-log than to miss one)
- Hear a requirement and start work without restating it first (the first line must be "Got it, next I'll do X")
- Leave a worktree undeleted when done (once merged/abandoned, remove + prune + delete the local branch in the same wrap-up; never create one in a sibling directory or `/tmp`)
- Give a review-round fix its own independent `fix:` commit while the unit hasn't merged into main yet (use `git commit --squash=<unit's first commit sha>` instead; independent `fix:` commits are only for post-merge production feedback)
- Let `docs/Progress.md`'s changelog pile up past the current month unarchived (archive last month's entries into `docs/archive/Progress-<YYYY-MM>.md` at the first batch wrap-up of each month)
- Leave processes started for tests running after testing ends (dev servers, browsers, containers; failure paths must shut them down too), or mass-kill processes by name
- Touch the request path, jobs, external calls, or health checks without writing log lines per the log contract (§4)

